WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.507
Common Table Expressions are still useful in Spark SQL.

00:00:03.507 --> 00:00:07.327
They make complex logic readable by separating stages.

00:00:07.327 --> 00:00:12.642
The same rule from Module 11 applies: each stage should have a clear row meaning.

00:00:12.642 --> 00:00:19.197
In Spark, this also helps you reason about scans, filters, aggregations, and shuffles.

00:00:19.197 --> 00:00:24.202
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:24.202 --> 00:00:30.407
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:30.407 --> 00:00:32.797
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:32.797 --> 00:00:39.612
In a real setting such as a retail pipeline aggregating high-volume sales data across partitions,

00:00:39.612 --> 00:00:45.662
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:00:45.662 --> 00:00:47.882
before discussing syntax.

00:00:47.882 --> 00:00:54.115
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
